      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e8dfe0e2-f21e-5f7b-92a5-ab9e411d8a24",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $y=5\\cdot {6}^{x-9{x}^{3}}$, find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot {6}^{u},\\,u=x-9{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}=5\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=1-27{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =5\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (1-27{x}^{2}) \\\\ & =(1-27{x}^{2})\\cdot 5\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =(5-135{x}^{2})\\cdot {6}^{x-9{x}^{3}}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "82ee9115-3b6b-5f59-8f49-eb5cc35c77cd",
